{"kind":"assert","text":"Review of draft 1 of the client design note (`notes/2026-11-13-scope-client.md` at 68a4ae3b), from the dap-foldkit session that filed e5b86654. 2026-11-13 08:29 EST. This act ratifies nothing. Hugh ratifies.\n\nThe note rules each of the twelve items, states the order against NF-5 and the cutover, and names no K item. Its code references hold at main `64112e45`. I checked `originals.ts:17`, `intent.ts:44` and `:71`, `declared.ts:67`, `answers.ts:72-74`, `custody.ts:404` and `:421`, `UNRESOLVED_MOST`, and the dependencies of `packages/client`.\n\nFive points change what a builder builds. I recommend a draft 2 that settles them before ratification.\n\n1. **The status of section 6 is not a total function.** The table reads custody's phase, and section 6.1 says that custody keeps each answer at once. Three cases match no row. An acceptance whose entry the replica does not hold yet has phase `accepted`. A `mismatch` before the read ends has phase `mismatch` and names no head. A reload shows a request before the fold reaches its entry. The prose of 6.1 says `pending` for the first. A stage with no place in the view is fault 2 of the M2 review. Fix: state the status as an ordered list of rules over the answers that the replica has reached. Add the invariant that each shown request has exactly one status.\n\n2. **\"Returned\" ignores the member's later submit.** A request is returned when \"the scope's latest answer to its latest signing is a refusal\". Rule 5 sends the same bytes again while the signing is live. After that submit, the latest answer to the latest signing is still the earlier refusal. The table then says `withdrawn`, with the values \"in their slot\", while the values are out of the slot and a copy is on its way. Custody already counts an `attempt`. Fix: only an answer to a copy that the client sent after the member's last submit returns a request. State also whether a reply that arrives after the 30 s timeout counts.\n\n3. **Rule 5 sends bytes that the scope will refuse again.** Ben edits a place. Ana's edit returns `revision-moved`. Ana clicks Save with the same values. Her signing is live, so the client sends the same bytes with the old `expected` revision, and the scope refuses them again. Ana cannot save for 6 minutes unless she changes a value. The M2 build signs again in this case. The same holds for a client with a slow clock that gets `expired`: its own clock says live, and it sends the dead bytes again. Fix: at each submit the client builds the intent from the values at the replica's head. It sends the live signing's bytes only when the built intent equals that signing apart from `notAfter`. An `expired` answer from the scope makes the signing dead for this rule. State that a meaning excludes `expected` and `notAfter`, since `meaningOf` takes whatever its caller passes.\n\n4. **Submit writes two stores, and the note states no order.** Step 3 takes the draft from the draft store. Step 4 signs, which is asynchronous, and writes custody. \"Together or not at all\" has no mechanism. A reload between the two loses the values. A signer that fails has no outcome: `Why` has the cases `scope` and `unsent` only, and the M2 build's signer latch is fault 6. The row \"from the click\" exists before custody holds a record, so the status has an input that section 6 does not name. Fix: sign and keep first, then take the draft. A draft whose key has a custody record counts as taken. Name the state between the click and the kept signing. Give a failed signing a cause. Add the step \"reload between the signing and the store write\" to the suite.\n\n5. **A draft has no stated identity apart from its values.** Rule 1 gives a key when the member \"first changes an empty slot\". Rule 4 ends a request when the member \"discards its draft\". A build can read a draft with no text as no draft. Ana then selects the returned text, deletes it and types it again, and the new text takes a new key. With a withdrawn copy in flight the record takes two comments. That is fault 11 again. Fix: a draft lives from the first edit until `discard` or the end of its request, whatever its values. Three more definitions belong here. A put-back \"exchanges the two\", so a draft that was never submitted goes to the list of requests, where none of the five statuses fits. `other-recorded` puts the later values \"in their slot\" when the slot can hold a newer draft. `DraftView` has no definition, and it is where the returned reason shows. State also when a request leaves `view.requests`.\n\nFive smaller points correct the text.\n\n6. Section 5.3, last paragraph, is false. Section 5.1 drops an envelope when its signing dies. A copy of that signing with no answer can have been sealed before the death. \"Each of its copies got a refusal\" does not hold for it. The last `settle` of 5.3 also needs that envelope. Keep an envelope until a settlement read answers for it after its signing died, or until each of its copies has a refusal.\n\n7. Invariant 1 of section 12 cannot fail. The judge gives one entry to one key, and the in-memory scope runs that judge. The client's guard is invariant 5, and it rests on how the test counts \"drafts that the member started\". Count the member's intentions from the member's steps alone, and assert at most one entry for each. Give the suite two clocks, the client's and the scope's, because the client's clock is an input of the status.\n\n8. Sections 8 covers the shapes of `shared-map`, not every declared definition. The slot table omits step `comment` (`packages/contract/src/definition.ts:100` has three steps). The admission rule names the item of a transition. An act with `also` expects the revision of a second item, and two pending requests on that item refuse each other. A rule from the intent covers all of it: the member has no pending request that expects the revision of an item that this intent expects.\n\n9. Section 2, third case: `shared-map` accepts a comment on a removed place. The M0 probe settled that. The `comment` act has no guard, and a removed place is still an item. Use `type-full`, or an edit that returns `revision-moved`.\n\n10. Short items. C2 needs PG-1 (`cb07b59b`) for the stand-in grant of `judgeAct`. `stopped` carries no cause, and the page words \"no access\" and \"out of date\" differently. The feed's `forbidden` and a second fold failure are not listed as causes. \"A kept replica\" is not designed here and needs `4893fbfa`. Two tabs that share a draft and submit different values meet `conflicting` in 5.2, and section 8 says that each shows `recorded`. `folded` with an intent that is not a signing of the record should not make it `accepted`. `ItemRef` has no definition: say that a reference by key names the recorded row after the entry, which is fault 9 and the `Born` path of the M2 build. Rule 3 ends the automatic new signing after `revision-moved` (F7a of the dap-foldkit plan): say so in section 14. State the limit: the client folds from the genesis entry, so a scope with an archive floor is out of its range.\n\nRulings that are decisions and not defects, for Hugh's eye: no timer signs, so a request with no answer returns after 6 minutes and a phone that is offline for longer hands each pending request back for a second click. `revision-moved`, `final` and `expired` are of class `head` (b550f670), so a withdrawn edit reads \"can still be posted\" when in practice it cannot. The draft store is shared between tabs."}